{"name":"Damson Mauve","hex":"#583563","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"damson","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A subspecies of plum tree, Prunus domestica subsp. insititia, native to Eurasia.","The edible fruit of this tree."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["The color of the fruit of this tree, a very deep purple."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/damson"},{"term":"mauve","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rich purple synthetic dye, which faded easily, briefly popular c. 1859‒1873 and now called mauveine.","A pale purple or violet colour, like the colour of the dye after it has faded."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a pale purple colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mauve"}],"entries":[{"title":"Damson","blocks":[{"text":"The damson, damson plum, or damascene, is an edible drupaceous fruit, a subspecies of the plum tree. Varieties of insititia are found across Europe, but the name damson is derived from and most commonly applied to forms that are native to Great Britain. Damsons are small, ovoid, plum-like fruit with a distinctive, somewhat astringent taste, and are widely used for culinary purposes, particularly in fruit preserves and jams."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Damson_plum_fruit.jpg/330px-Damson_plum_fruit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damson","wikidata":"Q10878191"},{"title":"Mauve","blocks":[{"text":"Mauve is a pale purple color named after the mallow flower. The first use of the word mauve as a color was in 1796–1798 according to the Oxford English Dictionary, but its use seems to have been rare before 1859. Another name for the color is mallow, with the first recorded use of mallow as a color name in English in 1611."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Wilde_Malve.JPG/330px-Wilde_Malve.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mauve","wikidata":"Q604079"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG/500px-Cvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG/1280px-Cvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG/500px-Cvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Damson flowers","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACvet_%C5%A1ljive_2.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Darwin_Domestic_143.png/500px-Darwin_Domestic_143.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Darwin_Domestic_143.png/500px-Darwin_Domestic_143.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Comparison of plum stones: Shropshire damson shown top row, second from left (no. 2). From Charles Darwin's Variation of Animals and Plants under Domestication","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADarwin_Domestic_143.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Page_14_plum_-_Imperial_Gage%2C_Shropshire_Damson%2C_Lombard%2C_Maynard%2C_Yellow_Egg.jpg/500px-Page_14_plum_-_Imperial_Gage%2C_Shropshire_Damson%2C_Lombard%2C_Maynard%2C_Yellow_Egg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Page_14_plum_-_Imperial_Gage%2C_Shropshire_Damson%2C_Lombard%2C_Maynard%2C_Yellow_Egg.jpg/1280px-Page_14_plum_-_Imperial_Gage%2C_Shropshire_Damson%2C_Lombard%2C_Maynard%2C_Yellow_Egg.jpg?utm_s